ARGOS

Out of your own hound, the spring before you sailed. You picked
him from the litter yourself: the one that would not settle, the
one that went for the biggest of the others.

You never hunted him. He was not old enough by the time the
ships went.

The young men in the house took him out after wild goat, after
hare, after deer, for a few seasons. Then they stopped bothering
with him.

He lies on the dung heap outside the gate, where the mule
manure is piled up until somebody carts it out to the fields.
Full of ticks. Too weak to get off it.

Nobody has changed anything about this dog in twenty years.
